oadCustomerHistory();\n166:       } catch (err) {\n167:         showToast(err.message, 'danger');\n168:       }\n169:     }\n170:   </script>\n171: </body>\n172: </html>\n173: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":547,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T20:24:08Z","thinking":"**Improving History Page Resilience**\n\nThe goal is to make the `history.html` page more robust. A critical aspect is handling 401 errors (unauthorized). Upon encountering a 401 status, the stale token should be cleared from storage. An informative alert must be displayed to the user, indicating session expiration or login requirements.\n\n\n**Implementing Session Expiration Handling**\n\nThe provided snippet showcases handling a login session expiration scenario.  A \"1-Click Customer Login\" button is being implemented that triggers an auto-login function, providing a seamless user experience upon session expiration. The auto-login function utilizes an API call to re-authenticate the user, stores the new token, and redirects if authentication fails.\n\n\n**Generating Dynamic Placeholder Content**\n\nHandling different \"no data\" states within the history page, a dynamic approach is needed. Conditional HTML blocks are constructed, each tailored to a specific data type: purchases, verifications, or combined records. The snippet utilizes distinct `<tr>` elements with informative messages and corresponding action links for each condition.\n\nThe logic dynamically renders the correct \"no data\" message and CTA depending on the absence of a certain record type within the page. This approach ensures informative display for the user.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\scratch\\\\medcheck-bd\\\\se